<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Seon Buddhism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Korean Seon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zen">Seon</a> is the Korean form of Chan or Zen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, which stresses meditation and direct insight. Introduced from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a> in the eighth and ninth centuries, it spread through the Nine Mountain schools of late Silla. In Goryeo the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinul">Jinul</a> joined it with doctrinal study, and it remains the main tradition of Korean Buddhism through the Jogye Order.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/seon-buddhism.jpg" alt="Seon Buddhism" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francisco Anzola · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jogyesa_Temple_(1509839597).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
